<Item XName="GlobalEqns" Attrib="14" Type="Equation">
<Text>FREQ=1e6;&#x000D;&#x000A;TIME=0.0;&#x000D;&#x000A;TEMPERATURE=16.85;&#x000D;&#x000A;Start_Time=0;&#x000D;&#x000A;setdisplayunit(Start_Time, &apos;s&apos;);&#x000D;&#x000A;Stop_Time=1;&#x000D;&#x000A;setdisplayunit(Stop_Time, &apos;s&apos;);&#x000D;&#x000A;Time_Spacing=.001;&#x000D;&#x000A;setdisplayunit(Time_Spacing, &apos;s&apos;);&#x000D;&#x000A;Num_Samples=1001;&#x000D;&#x000A;Sample_Rate=1000;&#x000D;&#x000A;setdisplayunit(Sample_Rate, &apos;Hz&apos;);&#x000D;&#x000A;Freq_Resolution=1;&#x000D;&#x000A;setdisplayunit(Freq_Resolution, &apos;Hz&apos;);&#x000D;&#x000A;Data_Persistence=0;&#x000D;&#x000A;Start_Time_SC=0;&#x000D;&#x000A;setdisplayunit(Start_Time_SC, &apos;s&apos;);&#x000D;&#x000A;Stop_Time_SC=1;&#x000D;&#x000A;setdisplayunit(Stop_Time_SC, &apos;s&apos;);&#x000D;&#x000A;Num_Samples_SC=1001;&#x000D;&#x000A;</Text>
<Item XName="BPMgr" Type="BPMgr">
<BPLines><T>Empty</T></BPLines>
<BPStates><T>Empty</T></BPStates></Item>
<Item XName="EqnBlock" Type="EqnBlock">
<Lang><T>Int</T><D>1</D></Lang></Item>
<Item XName="Font" Type="Font">
<Name>Courier New</Name>
<Size><T>Double</T><D>125</D></Size></Item>
<Item XName="FunctionBlock" Type="FunctionBlock" />
<Item XName="VarBlock" Type="VarBlock" /></Item>
<Item XName="Note1" UserName="Note1" Type="Notes">
<Selected><T>Bool</T><D>0</D></Selected>
<Text>&lt;P class=MsoTitle style=&quot;BORDER-TOP: medium none; BORDER-RIGHT: medium none; BORDER-BOTTOM: windowtext 0.75pt solid; PADDING-BOTTOM: 1pt; PADDING-TOP: 0in; PADDING-LEFT: 0in; BORDER-LEFT: medium none; MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt; PADDING-RIGHT: 0in&quot; align=center&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;FONT size=5 face=Arial&gt;Phased Array with Active Loading &lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;TEXT-ALIGN: center; MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; align=center&gt;&lt;EM&gt;&lt;FONT size=3&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 10pt; FONT-FAMILY: Arial; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt&quot;&gt;Keysight Technologies SystemVue Workspace&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;?xml:namespace prefix = &quot;o&quot; ns = &quot;urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office&quot; /&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/EM&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;TEXT-ALIGN: center; MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; align=center&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;EM&gt;&lt;FONT size=3&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/EM&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; align=center&gt;&lt;EM&gt;&lt;/EM&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; align=center&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 10pt; FONT-FAMILY: Arial; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;EM&gt;&lt;/EM&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;EM&gt;&lt;/EM&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;This workspace shows the effect of antenna array coupling and active loading on the final beam pattern.&amp;nbsp; For the ideal case, the resulting far field is just a linear sum of all the individual elements and their weighting factors.&amp;nbsp; But in realistic arrays, the array itself has internal coupling, and this is often&amp;nbsp;used in phased array&amp;nbsp;design.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;Loading can be specified a few different ways using the &quot;&lt;STRONG&gt;ActiveLoading&lt;/STRONG&gt;&quot; parameter on the &quot;&lt;STRONG&gt;ArrayAnt&lt;/STRONG&gt;&quot; component.&amp;nbsp; &lt;FONT size=2&gt;By switching different options, you can compare the beam measurement results in the tables.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;OL&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&lt;FONT size=2 face=Arial&gt;Most Ideal case:&amp;nbsp; You can set&amp;nbsp;the &quot;ActiveLoading&quot; option to &quot;&lt;U&gt;Ideal Coupling Matrix&lt;/U&gt;&quot;. The coupling matrix is computed in the equation Equation1. It computes an idealized, non-physical coupling based on the distance between the elements (dB_per_m and deg_per_m = 10; play with these values to see the effect they have on beam measurements).&amp;nbsp; For example, if a signal appears on element 41, then a small amount of that signal will also be added to element 42, based&amp;nbsp;on how far apart they are.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&lt;FONT size=2 face=Arial&gt;A more accurate option is to&amp;nbsp;set ActiveLoading&amp;nbsp;= &quot;&lt;U&gt;S-Parameter Dataset&lt;/U&gt;&quot;. The&amp;nbsp;model&amp;nbsp;uses &quot;8x8_array_touchstone.s64p&quot;&amp;nbsp;dataset in the workspace tree.&amp;nbsp; An 8x8 patch antenna array was solved in the ADS Momentum field solver, and the S-parameters stored to file, and then imported into this workspace.&amp;nbsp; Each of the 64 patches can &quot;talk&quot; to the other 63 patches,&amp;nbsp;resulting in&amp;nbsp;a 64x64 S-matrix at one frequency, at one scan angle (4096 values!).&amp;nbsp; This is much more accurate, and&amp;nbsp;provides a&amp;nbsp;physical basis for the coupling.&amp;nbsp; But these matrixes can get large.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&lt;FONT size=2 face=Arial&gt;You can also set ActiveLoading =&quot;&lt;U&gt;Active Reflection Coefficients&lt;/U&gt;&quot;. The&amp;nbsp;model&amp;nbsp;uses &quot;activeRefCoefs&quot;&amp;nbsp;from Equation3&amp;nbsp;in the workspace tree.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&lt;/OL&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT color=#0000ff size=2&gt;Results with No Loading:&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;UL&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;DIV class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT size=2 face=Arial&gt;The &lt;STRONG&gt;PhasedArrayTxData&lt;/STRONG&gt; dataset was stored as a reference with no active loading.&amp;nbsp;Beam pattern is&amp;nbsp;displayed in the &lt;STRONG&gt;BeamPatternTx&lt;/STRONG&gt; 3D graph.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/DIV&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&lt;/UL&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;&lt;FONT color=#0000ff&gt;Results with Loading:&lt;/FONT&gt; &lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;UL&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&lt;FONT size=2 face=Arial&gt;The schematic and simulation can be modified, and will output to the &lt;STRONG&gt;PhasedArrayTxActiveLoading &lt;/STRONG&gt;dataset.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;A default one has been stored with this&amp;nbsp;workslace. It&amp;nbsp;was created with&amp;nbsp;active loading&amp;nbsp;applied. Its beam pattern is&amp;nbsp;displayed in&amp;nbsp;&lt;STRONG&gt;BeamPatternTxActiveLoading&lt;/STRONG&gt;.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&lt;FONT size=2 face=Arial&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;SampleMeasurements&lt;/STRONG&gt; and &lt;STRONG&gt;SideLobeLevels&lt;/STRONG&gt; tables show sample measurements and sidelobe levels from both simulations for comparison purposes.&amp;nbsp; Note that with coupling, the deep notches fill in a little bit, among other changes.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&lt;FONT size=2 face=Arial&gt;Another effect of the coupling is to modify the impedance seen &quot;looking&quot; into each antenna array element. We are referring to this as the &lt;STRONG&gt;Active Impedance&lt;/STRONG&gt;. Active Impedance is saved in the dataset in the variable &lt;EM&gt;PartInfo_AntennaZinActive_&amp;lt;ArrayAntPartInstanceName&amp;gt; &lt;/EM&gt;(this variable is &lt;STRONG&gt;ONLY&lt;/STRONG&gt; available in Phased Array simulations in &lt;STRONG&gt;Tx&lt;/STRONG&gt; mode and when &lt;STRONG&gt;Active Loading&lt;/STRONG&gt; is set to &lt;EM&gt;S-Parameter File&lt;/EM&gt; or &lt;EM&gt;S-Parameter Dataset&lt;/EM&gt;). Active Impedance depends on the excitation of each element and so it &lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;FONT size=2 face=Arial&gt;changes with scan angle (as the excitations change to make the beam point to a different direction). The graph &lt;STRONG&gt;Active Impedance&lt;/STRONG&gt; shows the Active Impedances of each antenna array element. Tune &lt;EM&gt;ArrayPhase2.Phi&lt;/EM&gt; and &lt;EM&gt;ArrayPhase2.Theta&lt;/EM&gt; in the &lt;STRONG&gt;Tune Window&lt;/STRONG&gt; and observe how the active impedances change. Make sure &lt;STRONG&gt;PhasedArrayTx &lt;/STRONG&gt;analysis is checked in the &lt;STRONG&gt;Analyses To Run&lt;/STRONG&gt; area inside the &lt;STRONG&gt;Tune Window&lt;/STRONG&gt;.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&lt;/UL&gt;</Text>
